<!-- §01 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Homogenization Problem</div>
<h2>Shared Memory Collapses Agent Diversity</h2>
<p>Multi-agent systems (MAS) derive their power from <em>specialization</em>: a Planner decomposes problems differently than a Coder, who reasons differently than a Reviewer. But the dominant memory design pattern — inherited directly from single-agent architectures — is a <strong>centralized shared repository</strong> where every agent reads from, and writes to, the same pool.</p>
<p>The consequence is subtle but devastating: as agents accumulate experience, they all retrieve the same memory pieces for similar tasks. Over time, their behaviors converge. The Planner starts acting like the Coder. The Reviewer mirrors both. The system collapses toward a single dominant strategy — as if you only had one agent.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">strategy-space.canvas — agent behavior over tasks</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag the slider to see behavior after N tasks</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;align-items:center;margin-bottom:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <label style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Tasks elapsed:</label>
