<p>The <strong>E-pool (Exploitation pool)</strong> stores consolidated experience from past tasks: each memory piece records not just <em>what</em> was solved, but <em>how</em> — the full collaboration trajectory, intermediate reasoning steps, and the agent's self-commentary explaining its choices. Retrieval is similarity-based: given a new task, the agent embeds it and fetches the top-K most similar past pieces that exceed a similarity threshold τ.</p>
<p>The <strong>X-pool (Exploration pool)</strong> is a temporary buffer that generates fresh LLM candidates for the current task — novel strategies the agent has never tried before. It serves as a teleportation mechanism that injects probability mass into unexplored strategy regions, preventing the agent from getting permanently trapped by its own history.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">E-pool retrieval (similarity-based)</div>
  <span class="math-var">ρ</span> = Top-K(M<sub>E</sub> ; sim(e(x), e(ξ))) &nbsp;&nbsp; subject to sim ≥ <span class="math-var">τ</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// if no piece scores ≥ τ, fall back to X-pool generation</span>
  <br>
  <div class="math-label" style="margin-top:1rem">memory piece structure</div>
  z = (<span class="math-var">ξ</span>, r*)&nbsp;&nbsp; where r* = (<span class="math-var">r_trajectory</span>, <span class="math-var">r_comment</span>)
  <br><span class="math-comment">// ξ = context prototype, r_trajectory = what was done + coordination history,</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// r_comment = why this action was chosen (self-commentary)</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-router" data-code="router">
<div class="section-no">03 — Online Routing</div>
<h2>Learning the Exploitation-Exploration Balance</h2>
<p>The key question each agent faces at every task: should it <strong>exploit</strong> past experience (E-pool) or <strong>explore</strong> new strategies (X-pool)? A fixed schedule (always exploit, or always 50/50) leaves performance on the table. DecentMem learns the optimal balance online.</p>
<p>The routing probability is computed from the current pool weights. The E-pool starts at weight w_E = 1.0 (same as X-pool's fixed w_X = 1.0), giving α = 0.5 — equal odds. After each task, the LLM-as-a-judge evaluates each stage of the solution trajectory. If stage score improves (Δt = 1), the pool that was used at that stage gets a reward; otherwise it gets a penalty.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">online-router.sim — see weights update after each task</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click "run task" to simulate; watch α converge</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="router-ui">
      <div class="pool-gauges">
        <div class="pool-gauge">
          <div class="pool-gauge-label" style="color:var(--purple)">E-POOL weight</div>
          <div class="pool-gauge-val" id="we-val" style="color:var(--purple-l)">1.0</div>
          <div class="pool-gauge-bar"><div class="pool-gauge-fill" id="we-bar" style="background:var(--purple);width:50%"></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pool-gauge">
          <div class="pool-gauge-label" style="color:var(--green)">X-POOL weight</div>
          <div class="pool-gauge-val" style="color:#6ee7b7">1.0</div>
          <div class="pool-gauge-bar"><div class="pool-gauge-fill" style="background:var(--green);width:50%"></div></div>
          <div style="font-size:.6rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono);margin-top:.3rem">fixed (always 1.0)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pool-gauge">
          <div class="pool-gauge-label" style="color:var(--amber)">routing prob (α)</div>
          <div class="pool-gauge-val" id="alpha-val" style="color:var(--amber)">0.50</div>
          <div class="pool-gauge-bar"><div class="pool-gauge-fill" id="alpha-bar" style="background:var(--amber);width:50%"></div></div>
          <div style="font-size:.6rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono);margin-top:.3rem">P(use E-pool)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="router-alpha">α = w_E / (w_E + w_X) = <strong id="alpha-formula">1.0 / 2.0 = 0.50</strong></div>
      <div style="font-size:.67rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.4rem">Task history (purple=E-pool used, green=X-pool; filled=success, empty=fail):</div>
      <div class="router-history" id="router-history"></div>
      <div class="router-last" id="router-last">→ press "run task" to simulate</div>
      <div class="router-btn-row">
        <button class="router-btn" id="run-task-btn">▶ run task</button>
        <button class="router-btn" id="run-10-btn">▶▶ run 10 tasks</button>
        <button class="router-btn reset-btn" id="router-reset-btn">↺ reset</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The update rule is asymmetric by design. When exploitation succeeds, the agent doubles down (w_E increases). When exploitation fails, the agent hedges (w_E decays but never below 1.0). When exploration succeeds, the system <em>suppresses</em> E-pool dominance — successful exploration is a signal to stay curious, not to immediately consolidate. When exploration fails, the agent retreats to past experience.</p>
<p>Concretely, after every completed task, <strong>all X-pool pieces are consolidated into the E-pool</strong>, and the X-pool resets. The E-pool thus grows monotonically, accumulating successful explorations as new memories.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-theory" data-code="theory">
<div class="section-no">04 — Theoretical Guarantees</div>
<h2>Global Reachability and O(log T) Regret</h2>
<p>DecentMem comes with two formal guarantees that answer the core design questions: (1) can every agent always escape a bad strategy? and (2) how quickly does the routing converge to optimal?</p>

<h3>Theorem 1: Global Reachability</h3>
<p>Model each agent's strategy space as a graph G_m, where nodes are candidate strategies and edges connect similar strategies. The E-pool defines a <em>local walk</em> — the agent moves between similar strategies. Alone, this walk can get trapped: the agent replays good-but-suboptimal strategies forever.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">reachability.canvas — local walk vs dual-pool teleportation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle modes to see how X-pool enables escape</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="reach-controls">
      <button class="reach-btn active" id="reach-btn-eonly">E-pool only (stuck)</button>
      <button class="reach-btn" id="reach-btn-dual">Dual-pool (free)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="reach-canvas" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="reach-caption" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-top:.6rem;text-align:center">E-pool local walk: agent can only move along solid edges — trapped in the red cluster</div>
    <div class="reach-legend">
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#ef4444"></span>trapped region (suboptimal)</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#10b981"></span>optimal region</span>
      <span><span class="dot" style="background:#6b7280"></span>unexplored</span>
      <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">— local edge &nbsp;&nbsp; ⋯ X-pool teleport</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The X-pool fixes this. Because it generates fresh LLM candidates — which always carry nonzero probability mass over every feasible strategy — it acts as a <em>heuristic teleportation</em> mechanism. The combined transition matrix becomes strictly positive (every entry &gt; 0), making the Markov chain irreducible. <strong>No agent can ever be permanently trapped.</strong></p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 1 (Global Reachability)</div>
  M<sub>ℓ</sub> = <span class="math-var">α</span><sub>ℓ</sub> T + (1 − <span class="math-var">α</span><sub>ℓ</sub>) h 1<sup>⊤</sup>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// T = E-pool local-walk matrix, h = X-pool LLM prior (h_i > 0 for all i)</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// Since α_ℓ &lt; 1 and h_i > 0 → M is strictly positive → Markov chain is irreducible</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// ∴ agent am can reach any node in G_m from any starting point</span>
</div>

<h3>Theorem 2: O(log T) Regret</h3>
<p>The routing between E-pool and X-pool is a stochastic bandit problem: at each round, the router picks an arm (E or X), observes a reward, and updates weights. The update rule of DecentMem drives α toward the optimal balance α* — the routing probability that maximizes expected task reward. The theoretical bound is order-optimal:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 2 (Logarithmic Regret)</div>
  E[R(T)] = O(log T)
  <br><span class="math-comment">// R(T) = Σ_&#123;t=1&#125;^&#123;T&#125; [r(α*) − E[r(α_t)]]  ← cumulative loss vs optimal routing</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// Lower bound: any policy incurs Ω(log T) regret on some instance [Lai & Robbins 1985]</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// ∴ DecentMem's routing is order-optimal — no algorithm can do better</span>
</div>
<p>This guarantees that each agent's exploitation-exploration balance converges to its own optimal — not a shared optimum forced by centralized policy, but the individual optimum shaped by that agent's unique role and experience.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">05 — Results</div>
<h2>Gains That Widen with Coordination Stochasticity</h2>
<p>DecentMem was evaluated across 3 MAS frameworks, 5 LLM backbones (Qwen3-4B/8B/14B, Gemma4-E2B/E4B), and 5 benchmarks (AIME25&amp;24, MBPP-Plus, ALFWorld, BBH). The strongest centralized baseline is G-Memory (hierarchical shared graph memory).</p>
<p>The headline finding: <strong>DecentMem's advantage grows monotonically as coordination becomes more stochastic</strong> — from pre-designed workflows (AutoGen) to dynamic selection (DyLAN) to opportunistic unstructured coordination (AgentNet).</p>

<h3>Accuracy gain over G-Memory (Qwen3 family, average across 5 benchmarks)</h3>
<div class="results-bars" id="bars-stoch">
  <div style="font-size:.67rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.3rem">framework → increasing stochasticity →</div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">AutoGen (structured)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="14" style="background:rgba(139,92,246,.35)"><span>+2.7%</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">DyLAN (dynamic)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="46" style="background:rgba(139,92,246,.6)"><span>+9.2%</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">AgentNet (opportunistic)</div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="100" style="background:var(--purple)"><span>+23.1%</span></div></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This pattern is the central validation of DecentMem's thesis: centralized memory is most harmful when agents need to diverge. In stochastic coordination, agents need to discover individually optimal strategies that vary by context — but shared memory homogenizes them. Private memory preserves those divergent trajectories.</p>

<h3>Token cost reduction vs G-Memory</h3>
<div class="results-bars" id="bars-tokens">
  <div style="font-size:.67rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.3rem">Qwen3-8B — fewer tokens while improving accuracy</div>
